# Sensitive Periods
#
# Windows when experience shapes development most, from imprinting and birdsong to ocular dominance and the winter memory of plants, with the human language case kept contested.

#>development_ecology_sensitive_periods
#:layer #!reference
#:domain #!development_ecology
#:form #!ledger
#:level #!foundation
#:review #!adversarial_checked
#:valence #!bonk

^"emit"{
 ~#title: "Sensitive Periods"
 ~#claim: "Many organisms have windows when experience shapes development most strongly; the windows are graded, can sometimes reopen, and for human language their edges are still argued."
 ~#proof: "Imprinting from Lorenz; song learning from Marler; ocular dominance from Wiesel and Hubel; vernalization genes from Amasino and colleagues; language debate from Johnson and Newport, Hakuta, and Hartshorne."
 ~#door: "Read the birds and kittens first, then the plants, then the contested human cases."
 ~#audience: "curious readers and students of development"
 continuity: "Sensitive Periods | windows are graded"
 cold_open: "Some lessons land hardest at certain ages, and those windows are graded, not slammed shut."
}

